{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 38, :comments ["<b>And Aharon shall bear.</b> A term of forgiveness; and even so it does not depart from its meaning — Aharon bears the burden of the offense — so the offense is removed from the sacred donations (Ḥullin 138)." "<b>The offense of the sacred donations.</b> To effect atonement for the blood and the fat that were offered in impurity, as we learned: Which offense does it bear? If the offense of pigul — behold it was already stated ״לֹא יֵרָצֶה״ (Leviticus 19); and if the offense of notar — behold it was stated ״לֹא יֵחָשֵׁב״ (ibid. 7). And one should not say that it atones for the offense of the priest who offered it impure, for behold 'the offense of the sacred donations' is stated and not 'the offense of the offerers' — so it effects atonement only to render the offering valid (Yoma 7)." "<b>And it shall be on his forehead always.</b> It is impossible to say that it is literally on his forehead always, for behold it is on him only during the service — rather, 'always' means it effects atonement for them even when it is not on his forehead — when the Kohen Gadol was not serving at that hour. And according to the one who says it effects atonement only while it is still on his forehead, and if not it does not effect atonement — 'on his forehead always' is interpreted to teach that he must keep touching it while it is on his forehead, so that he not divert his attention from it (ibid.)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:55:25.614059388Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}